<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>pygixml Benchmarks</title>
<style>
:root {
  --bg: #0b0e14;
  --bg-panel: #12161f;
  --bg-panel-2: #171c28;
  --border: #232938;
  --text: #e6e9ef;
  --text-dim: #8b93a7;
  --accent: #5b8cff;
  --accent-2: #35d0ba;
  --warn: #ffb454;
  --bad: #ff6b81;
  --good: #35d0ba;
  --mono: 'SFMono-Regular', Consolas, 'Liberation Mono', Menlo, monospace;
}
* { box-sizing: border-box; }
body {
  margin: 0;
  background: var(--bg);
  color: var(--text);
  font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
  line-height: 1.55;
}
.wrap { max-width: 1080px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 24px 80px; }

/* ---- Hero ---- */
.hero {
  padding: 90px 24px 70px;
  text-align: center;
  background:
    radial-gradient(ellipse 900px 500px at 50% -10%, rgba(91,140,255,0.20), transparent 60%),
    linear-gradient(180deg, var(--bg) 0%, var(--bg) 100%);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
}
.hero .eyebrow {
  font-size: 13px;
  letter-spacing: .14em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--accent-2);
  font-weight: 600;
  margin-bottom: 18px;
}
.hero h1 {
  font-size: clamp(28px, 4.4vw, 48px);
  line-height: 1.18;
  margin: 0 auto 20px;
  max-width: 880px;
  font-weight: 800;
  letter-spacing: -0.02em;
}
.hero h1 .hl { color: var(--accent); }
.hero p.sub {
  color: var(--text-dim);
  font-size: 17px;
  max-width: 660px;
  margin: 0 auto;
}
.hero .meta {
  margin-top: 28px;
  font-family: var(--mono);
  font-size: 12.5px;
  color: var(--text-dim);
}

/* ---- Sections ---- */
section { padding: 64px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
section:last-of-type { border-bottom: none; }
.kicker {
  font-size: 12.5px;
  letter-spacing: .12em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--accent-2);
  font-weight: 700;
  margin-bottom: 10px;
}
h2 { font-size: 27px; margin: 0 0 12px; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
h3.op-title { font-size: 18px; margin: 44px 0 6px; }
h3.op-title:first-of-type { margin-top: 8px; }
.lede { color: var(--text-dim); font-size: 16px; max-width: 740px; margin: 0 0 32px; }
.lede strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 650; }
.op-note { color: var(--text-dim); font-size: 13.5px; max-width: 740px; margin: 0 0 16px; }

.panel {
  background: var(--bg-panel);
  border: 1px solid var(--border);
  border-radius: 14px;
  padding: 26px;
  margin-bottom: 22px;
}
.panel h4 { margin: 0 0 4px; font-size: 14px; color: var(--text-dim); font-weight: 600; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .03em; }
.chart-box { position: relative; height: 320px; }
.chart-box.tall { height: 420px; }
.chart-pair { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 18px; }
@media (max-width: 780px) { .chart-pair { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }

/* ---- Tables ---- */
table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14px; }
th, td { text-align: left; padding: 10px 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
th { color: var(--text-dim); font-weight: 600; font-size: 12.5px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em; }
td.num, th.num { text-align: right; font-family: var(--mono); }
tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }

.badge {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  min-width: 22px;
  height: 22px;
  border-radius: 6px;
  font-size: 12px;
  font-weight: 700;
  padding: 0 6px;
}
.badge.yes { background: rgba(53,208,186,0.15); color: var(--good); }
.badge.no  { background: rgba(255,107,129,0.12); color: var(--bad); }
.badge.partial { background: rgba(255,180,84,0.15); color: var(--warn); }

.feature-table td:first-child { color: var(--text); font-weight: 550; white-space: nowrap; }
.feature-table .fnote { display:block; color: var(--text-dim); font-weight: 400; font-size: 12px; margin-top: 2px; }
.feature-table td, .feature-table th { text-align: center; }
.feature-table td:first-child, .feature-table th:first-child { text-align: left; }

/* ---- Section-local download ---- */
.section-download { margin-top: 22px; }
.dl-btn {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 8px;
  background: var(--bg-panel-2);
  border: 1px solid var(--border);
  color: var(--text);
  padding: 9px 14px;
  border-radius: 9px;
  font-size: 13px;
  font-family: var(--mono);
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: border-color .15s, background .15s;
}
.dl-btn:hover { border-color: var(--accent); background: #1a2030; }
.dl-btn svg { width: 14px; height: 14px; flex: none; }

footer {
  padding: 40px 0 20px;
  color: var(--text-dim);
  font-size: 13px;
  text-align: center;
}
footer .sysline { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 12px; margin-bottom: 10px; }
footer a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }
footer a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }

.legend-note { font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--text-dim); margin-top: 10px; }
code { font-family: var(--mono); background: var(--bg-panel-2); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 5px; font-size: 0.92em; }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="hero">
  <div class="eyebrow">pygixml benchmark report &middot; generated {{ generated_at }}</div>
  <h1>{{ headline | safe }}</h1>
  <p class="sub">{{ subhead | safe }}</p>
  <div class="meta">{{ meta_line }}</div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">

{% if has_memory %}
<!-- ========================= MEMORY ========================= -->
<section id="memory">
  <div class="kicker">Memory</div>
  <h2>Peak RSS vs. input size</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Every library here that builds a DOM (or an equivalent in-memory dict/tree)
    uses more RAM as the input file grows. <strong>pygixml.jsonify.stream_dump</strong>
    is the one approach that doesn't: it converts XML straight to JSON without ever
    holding the whole tree in memory, so its curve is flat &mdash; O(1) against the
    others' O(n). That's a difference in <em>growth rate</em>, not a fixed multiplier:
    the gap widens as input grows, so no single "N&times; less memory" number at one
    input size represents it fairly &mdash; the chart does.{{ memory_lede_extra }}
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h4>Peak RSS vs. input size</h4>
    <p class="op-note">Log scale on both axes &mdash; a flat line here means constant memory; a line with the same slope as the others means it scales with input, same as everyone else.</p>
    <div class="chart-box tall"><canvas id="chart-memory"></canvas></div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-download">
    <button class="dl-btn" onclick="downloadJSON('memory')">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 3v12m0 0l-4-4m4 4l4-4M4 21h16"/></svg>
      memory.json
    </button>
  </div>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if has_scaling %}
<!-- ========================= SCALING ========================= -->
<section id="scaling">
  <div class="kicker">Time complexity</div>
  <h2>The shape that flatters us, and the one that doesn't</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    <strong>stream_dump</strong> writes optimistically and patches the output
    file in place once it learns a tag repeats. For the shape almost all real
    giant XML actually has &mdash; one repeated tag per nesting level, like
    <code>&lt;orders&gt;&lt;order&gt;...&lt;/order&gt;...&lt;/orders&gt;</code>
    &mdash; that's O(n). We also generate the one shape where it isn't: two
    different tags repeating and interleaving at the same level. We're
    showing that curve here on purpose, not just citing it in the docs.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h4>Time vs. N, by shape</h4>
    <p class="op-note">Same operation (<code>jsonify.stream_dump</code>), two different input shapes. Log-log, so a straight line means a power-law relationship &mdash; the slope tells you which power.</p>
    <div class="chart-box tall"><canvas id="chart-scaling"></canvas></div>
    <p class="legend-note">{{ scaling_note | safe }}</p>
  </div>

  {% if scaling_dom_available %}
  <div class="panel">
    <h4>Records-shape XML &rarr; JSON, DOM-based approaches</h4>
    <p class="op-note">For reference: everyone's roughly-linear time on the realistic shape, at moderate N.</p>
    <div class="chart-box"><canvas id="chart-scaling-dom"></canvas></div>
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  <div class="section-download">
    <button class="dl-btn" onclick="downloadJSON('scaling')">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 3v12m0 0l-4-4m4 4l4-4M4 21h16"/></svg>
      scaling.json
    </button>
  </div>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if has_throughput %}
<!-- ========================= THROUGHPUT ========================= -->
<section id="throughput">
  <div class="kicker">Speed, at realistic sizes</div>
  <h2>Every conversion layer, not just JSON</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    pygixml is a set of independent layers on the same pugixml-backed core &mdash;
    raw DOM, dict (<code>dictify</code>), lazy object (<code>objectify</code>), and
    JSON (<code>jsonify</code>). Each gets its own panel below, measured against its
    real, direct competitor rather than being folded into a single "XML to JSON"
    number. Each panel has two charts: <strong>time</strong> per corpus entry (best
    of {{ repeats }} runs), and <strong>throughput</strong> (MB/s) vs. input size,
    to see whether the rate holds up as files grow.
  </p>

  {% for op in throughput_ops %}
  <h3 class="op-title">{{ op.title }}</h3>
  <p class="op-note">{{ op.note }}</p>
  <div class="chart-pair">
    <div class="panel">
      <h4>Time (ms, lower is better) &mdash; dot plot, log scale</h4>
      <div class="chart-box tall"><canvas id="chart-time-{{ op.key }}"></canvas></div>
    </div>
    {% if op.has_tp_chart %}
    <div class="panel">
      <h4>Throughput (MB/s, higher is better)</h4>
      <div class="chart-box"><canvas id="chart-tp-{{ op.key }}"></canvas></div>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
  {% endfor %}

  <div class="section-download">
    <button class="dl-btn" onclick="downloadJSON('throughput')">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 3v12m0 0l-4-4m4 4l4-4M4 21h16"/></svg>
      throughput.json
    </button>
  </div>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if has_sizes %}
<!-- ========================= SIZE ========================= -->
<section id="size">
  <div class="kicker">Footprint</div>
  <h2>What you actually download</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Real wheel sizes for the current platform, resolved directly through
    <a href="https://github.com/MohammadRaziei/pip-size" style="color:var(--accent)">pip-size</a>'s
    Python API (not its CLI) &mdash; no downloads, no installs, just querying PyPI's index.
    ElementTree needs nothing: it ships with Python itself.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="chart-box"><canvas id="chart-size"></canvas></div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-download">
    <button class="dl-btn" onclick="downloadJSON('sizes')">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 3v12m0 0l-4-4m4 4l4-4M4 21h16"/></svg>
      sizes.json
    </button>
  </div>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if has_features %}
<!-- ========================= FEATURES ========================= -->
<section id="features">
  <div class="kicker">Beyond speed</div>
  <h2>What each library can actually do</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Speed and memory aren't the whole story, and pygixml's scope isn't either
    (pygixml = <strong>py</strong>thon <strong>gi</strong>ant <strong>xml</strong>,
    built on pugixml plus a streaming yxml layer for the oversized case). This is
    a factual capability comparison, not a benchmark &mdash; see
    <code>benchmarks/python/features.py</code> for sourcing.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <table class="feature-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Feature</th>
          {% for lib in libraries %}<th>{{ lib_labels[lib] }}</th>{% endfor %}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for row in features %}
        <tr>
          <td>{{ row.feature }}{% if row.note %}<span class="fnote">{{ row.note }}</span>{% endif %}</td>
          {% for lib in libraries %}
          {% set cell = row['values'][lib] %}
          <td>
            <span class="badge {{ cell.value }}">{{ badge_symbol[cell.value] }}</span>
            {% if cell.detail %}<span class="fnote">{{ cell.detail }}</span>{% endif %}
          </td>
          {% endfor %}
        </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="section-download">
    <button class="dl-btn" onclick="downloadJSON('features')">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 3v12m0 0l-4-4m4 4l4-4M4 21h16"/></svg>
      features.json
    </button>
  </div>
</section>
{% endif %}

</div>

<footer>
  {% if system_info %}
  <div class="sysline">
    Run on {{ system_info.cpu_model }} &middot; {{ system_info.logical_cores }} logical cores
    {% if system_info.ram_gib %}&middot; {{ system_info.ram_gib }} GiB RAM{% endif %}
    &middot; {{ system_info.os }} &middot; {{ system_info.python_implementation }} {{ system_info.python_version }}
  </div>
  {% endif %}
  For how the corpus was built, why timing uses best-of-N, and the memory-measurement
  gotcha this report ran into, see
  <a href="https://github.com/MohammadRaziei/pygixml/blob/main/benchmarks/README.md">benchmarks/README.md</a>.
  <br>
  Generated by <code>benchmarks/report/generate_report.py</code> &middot;
  <a href="https://github.com/MohammadRaziei/pygixml">github.com/MohammadRaziei/pygixml</a>
</footer>

<script>window.PYGIXML_CHART_LIB_LOADING = true;</script>
<script>
{{ chartjs_source | safe }}
</script>

<script id="pygixml-data" type="application/json">
{{ embedded_json | safe }}
</script>

<script>
const DATA = JSON.parse(document.getElementById('pygixml-data').textContent);

function downloadJSON(name) {
  const blob = new Blob([JSON.stringify(DATA[name], null, 2)], {type: 'application/json'});
  const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
  const a = document.createElement('a');
  a.href = url; a.download = name + '.json';
  document.body.appendChild(a); a.click(); document.body.removeChild(a);
  URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
}

Chart.defaults.color = '#8b93a7';
Chart.defaults.borderColor = '#232938';
Chart.defaults.font.family = "-apple-system, 'Segoe UI', sans-serif";

{{ chart_scripts | safe }}
</script>
</body>
</html>
